## Imagine-mode disclaimer

This ticket is design dialogue. It proposes a theoretical thesis
that could become the paper's *fil rouge* (through-line) connecting
§1 baseline, §2 quality bar, §3 capability ladder, §4 SOTA frontier,
§5 tailored solution, and §6 conclusion. The idea is not yet
ratified into the manuscript; this ticket captures it for review.

## The thesis (author wording, 2026-05-22)

> Equipping an expert agent with a confidence-scale vocabulary
> alleviates the ambiguity-vs-recall trade-off. Without one, the
> agent has to crisp-cut between "I know and report" vs "unsure
> and better not say."

## Why this is load-bearing across §1–§6

**§1 baseline.** The parametric models in §1 face the binary choice
explicitly. We observe the failure modes (Récalcitrant, Hallucinant,
Incomplet, Non-déterministe, Non-monotone) precisely because the
model has only two choices when its parametric knowledge is
uncertain: fabricate confidently, or refuse. The confidence-band
vocabulary would let the model output *"likely Vũng Áng 2 is
operational"* instead of either *"yes operational"* or omission.

**§2 quality bar.** Accuracy / coherence / provenance / temporality
are currently framed as binary axes (a claim is or is not accurate,
sourced, dated). The four-dimensional bar would gain a *fifth
implicit dimension* — confidence — that modulates each. Strong
provenance (the cited URL fully supports the claim) is genuinely
different from weak provenance (the cited URL is in the right
neighbourhood); IPCC-style bands name that difference.

**§3 capability ladder.** Articulation / Coverage / Reasoning /
Tool use / Retroactivity / Agency are framed as orthogonal lifts.
Confidence vocabulary is a *cross-cutting* lift — a model that
ladders Coverage (via RAG) without confidence vocabulary still
faces the recall-precision binary on each retrieved fact; with
confidence vocabulary it can include lower-band facts as such.

**§4 SOTA frontier.** The 2026-05-21 v2-panel pilot (ticket 0223)
documented the failure mode directly: when the verify panel got
stricter, the polished inventory shrank from 43 to 26 plants. The
model dropped rows it could not verify rather than retaining them
with a band. The fil rouge says: *that drop is an artefact of
missing vocabulary*, not a quality lift. A confidence-band-equipped
verifier would have kept the 17 rows with "likely" or "about as
likely as not" labels.

**§5 tailored solution.** The IDH cycle (0224) already adopts IPCC
bands as a default (cross-cutting idea B). The fil rouge frames why:
the bands aren't decoration — they're the *operational instantiation*
of the theoretical contribution.

**§6 conclusion.** The paper's *to our knowledge* claim becomes:
"to our knowledge, no published evaluation of frontier statistical-
extraction agents equips them with a confidence-band vocabulary
across all four §2 dimensions, and we show empirically that the
absence forces a recall-precision trade-off that vanishes with the
vocabulary present."

## Empirical anchors already in the paper

Three existing observations support the thesis:

1. The 43→26 plant shrinkage between the v1 and v2 verify rounds
   in 0185's Mistral pilot (§4 First observation).
2. Claude Haiku's "consistently 17 plants" *Incomplet* failure
   mode in §1 — the model refuses to enumerate anything beyond a
   small high-confidence core.
3. Qwen 3.6-27B's "268 false positives" *Hallucinant* failure mode
   in §1 — the same lack of vocabulary pushed the other way: the
   model included low-confidence plants without labelling them.

Both Haiku and Qwen are responding to the same missing vocabulary
with opposite biases. The thesis predicts: equip both with bands,
both improve.

## What this is NOT (out of scope for this ticket)

- Not a new experiment. The thesis is *retrospective* — it
  reorganises observations already in the paper around a single
  theoretical claim.
- Not a re-implementation of Exp 1 or Exp 2. Those are locked.
- Not a §6 rewrite proposal — that follows once the thesis is
  ratified.

## If ratified, what changes in the manuscript

- §1: a paragraph naming the binary-choice failure mode and pointing
  forward to the vocabulary thesis.
- §2: an explicit "fifth implicit dimension — confidence" added
  to the four-dimensional bar, *or* a footnote arguing it's
  cross-cutting (the latter is lighter touch).
- §3: confidence vocabulary as a cross-cutting capability axis
  alongside the six ladder rungs (or its own §3.7).
- §4: the v2-panel observation reframed as evidence for the
  thesis, not as ambiguous about depth-vs-recall.
- §5: explicit adoption of IPCC bands (already planned in 0224).
- §6: the thesis statement as the paper's central claim.

## Open questions

- Choice of vocabulary: literal IPCC 7-band, or a 3-band simplification
  ("certain / likely / uncertain") for usability?
- Are there published philosophy-of-science / data-quality precedents
  for naming "the binary-choice failure mode under uncertainty" as a
  distinct category? If so, the literature review in §2 expands.
- Is the thesis falsifiable inside this paper, or does it require a
  follow-up paper that *measures* the recall improvement when bands
  are added? Likely the latter — this paper plants the flag.

## Status

Imagine-mode. Ratification = author edits §1–§6 to thread the thesis
through, after which this ticket closes "thesis adopted, see git
log on slides/manuscript/main.md."
